{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-88458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abf016f4f3224aef5599624969fb917d5733122d43aca6919c80fe4dbd98a9ec"} {"question" "q-88458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x}=6x\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =6x\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-6x\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x-45)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-5)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $3=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3)\\cdot 5=-15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abf016f4f3224aef5599624969fb917d5733122d43aca6919c80fe4dbd98a9ec"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-15"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-\\frac{9}{5}"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-88474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+4x-48){3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+2x-24) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+6)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abf016f4f3224aef5599624969fb917d5733122d43aca6919c80fe4dbd98a9ec"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264772" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+11x+28){5}^{2x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x+28=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{2x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+11x+28=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+7)(x+4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-7,-4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{2x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{2x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-7\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abf016f4f3224aef5599624969fb917d5733122d43aca6919c80fe4dbd98a9ec"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264780" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}=64\\cdot {e}^{x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-64)\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-64=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9{x}^{2}-64=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+8)(3x-8) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "abf016f4f3224aef5599624969fb917d5733122d43aca6919c80fe4dbd98a9ec"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"} {"question" "q-264788" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004555/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa5ee0546e28c104ef5c590031a6fee98213f48ce04072ea9a887542c7d5549b"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1096 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
